uVoiceIme is **proprietary software and is not open source**.

1. Source code
   The source code, build scripts and development documentation of this project
   are unpublished proprietary work of the author. No right is granted to copy,
   modify, compile, distribute, sublicense, publicly communicate or use them to
   create derivative works. They may not be passed on to a third party, in whole
   or in part, without the author's prior written permission.

2. Binary artefacts: free for individual users
   The uVoiceIme executable images published by the author (`*.efi` and their
   release packages) are **free for individual users for personal,
   non-commercial use**. Within that scope you may download, install and run
   them, and move copies between devices you own.

3. Commercial use requires a separate licence
   Any commercial use requires the author's prior written commercial licence,
   including but not limited to: distributing or preloading the software with a
   product, hardware or service; providing it as part of a paid service;
   deploying it in bulk or for production purposes inside a company, institution
   or organisation; and using it to deliver paid services such as outsourcing,
   integration or support.

4. Redistribution
   Binary artefacts may not be repackaged, renamed, split up, resold for a fee,
   or presented as someone else's work. Release packages must be passed on
   unmodified, with this licence file and the copyright notice retained.

5. Speech recognition service and network access
   This software contains no speech recognition engine of its own. Recognition
   depends on a third-party cloud service that you configure yourself (currently
   MiniMax's `asr-1.0` endpoint); you supply the account and the key, and you
   comply with that provider's terms. Costs, availability, data handling and
   recognition quality of that service are outside the scope of this licence,
   and the author's warranty and liability do not extend to it. Your key is kept
   in clear text in a configuration file on your own machine, and is yours to
   look after.

6. Data and privacy
   The audio being recognised is sent to the recognition service named in your
   configuration. The software collects nothing else, uploads nothing else, and
   contains no telemetry.

7. Third-party components
   The binaries include LVGL, the graphics library, and its UEFI port layer,
   both released under the MIT licence; their copyright belongs to their
   respective authors and the MIT terms are unaffected by this licence. EDK2,
   QEMU, OVMF and Visual Studio, used for building and verification, are
   independent open-source or commercial products under their own licences.

8. No warranty and limitation of liability
   This software is provided "as is", without warranties of any kind, express or
   implied, including but not limited to merchantability, fitness for a
   particular purpose and non-infringement. In no event shall the author be
   liable for any direct, indirect, incidental or consequential damages arising
   from the use of or inability to use this software, including data loss,
   hardware damage, business interruption or losses caused by recognition
   errors.

9. Termination
   A breach of any of the terms above terminates the rights granted by this
   licence automatically. On termination you must stop using the software and
   delete all copies.
